--[[
	Anomaly Coop - callback router, Lua half (gamedata\scripts\coop_route.script; docs\MOD_LAYER.md 2.2)

	Anomaly's callback dispatcher is Lua (axr_main.callback_set / make_callback), so a thin shim stays here. It wraps
	every handler once, when it is registered, with the domain the engine gives it (coop.route.of, coop_router.cpp).
	The rules are the engine's (xrCoop\coop_routes.h, configs\coop_route*.ltx): nothing here decides a domain.
		host  - only on the host        owner, view - everywhere        none - nowhere in a session (the host too)
	Outside a session every handler runs (single player is the stock game). An owner or view handler runs on every
	machine in every phase (routes::runs_here), so its wrapper does not ask; a host, level or none handler asks the
	engine on every call whether its domain runs on this machine (coop.route.runs).

	Installed on every machine with the engine API, the host and single player included (a session can start in this
	Lua state): coop_main calls install() when it loads, which axr_main.on_game_start does before any script's
	on_game_start runs. Wrapped:
		axr_main.callback_set / callback_unset   (RegisterScriptCallback / UnregisterScriptCallback)
		_G.AddUniqueCall / RemoveUniqueCall      a skipped call returns false: it stays registered
		_G.CreateTimeEvent and demonized_time_events.CreateTimeEvent: a skipped event returns true, so it is removed
	Kept as the stock dispatcher has it:
	- one wrapper per (callback, function), reused when the pair is registered again and found when it is unregistered
	  (by the function or by the wrapper itself);
	- axr_main's priority value of each handler (its registration order): a handler registered before install is
	  replaced by its wrapper in place, with the same value;
	- a table or userdata handler is called obj[name](obj, ...), the method looked up on every call;
	- make_callback is not replaced: coop_sound.cpp reads its upvalue `intercepts`.
	On a co-op client a handler that runs is called under pcall: a Lua error is logged (the first three per script,
	then every 100th) and counted, the session goes on; a failed unique call or time event returns true, so
	it is not called again. The 30th error of a script is told to the player once, as news (st_coop_route_mod_failing).
	The host and single player keep the stock behaviour (no pcall).
	Every call is counted by the engine's router counters, shown by coop_status: ran, skipped, error per domain, skips per
	script. A skip and an error are told at once (coop.route.note); the runs of owner and view handlers are counted here and
	handed over in one call (coop.route.note_batch) once a second and before this file's status line, so the engine's ran
	counts of those two domains lag by at most a second.

	Code scope: the engine's writer gate asks which code a script write comes from. Every
	wrapper is registered with the engine (coop.route.scoped(wrapper, domain)) and stays on the Lua stack while its handler runs
	(no tail call, also on the host), so the engine reads the scope from the live stack: the innermost registered wrapper. A
	handler's wrapper has the handler's domain. A time event and a unique call keep the scope of the code that set them
	(kept_scope: host where either the setter or the function's own domain is host; the setter's level, owner or view domain
	where the function also runs on a map server; else "?", unknown). An error leaves nothing stale: the scope is the stack.

	Tick service: every(ms, fn) runs fn every ms milliseconds of game time from one actor_on_update of this file, which reads
	the clock once per frame. fn keeps everything its own actor_on_update registration would give it: it is routed by its
	own script and domain (coop.route.of("actor_on_update", fn)), called through its own wrapper (its scope, its skips and
	errors per script, the client's pcall), and runs on its first frame. A frame where it is not due costs it nothing.

	API:
		coop_route.install() -> bool                   idempotent; false on an exe without coop.route
		coop_route.on_game_start()                     wraps AddUniqueCall / RemoveUniqueCall / CreateTimeEvent again
		                                               when a script replaced them after install (_g_patches.script
		                                               sets its own _G.CreateTimeEvent)
		coop_route.handlers_of(callback, script) -> { fn, ... }   the script's own handlers of a callback, in priority
		                                               order (script key as coop.route.of gives it, e.g. "treasure_manager")
		coop_route.wrapper_of(callback, fn) -> wrapper | nil
		coop_route.intercepts() -> axr_main's callback table | nil
		coop_route.script_of(fn) -> script key ("?" without the router)
		coop_route.inherit_source(wrapper, fn)         route a compatibility wrapper as the handler it replaces
		coop_route.handler_domain(fn) -> domain of a function registered as a handler, nil for any other function
		coop_route.kept_scope(setter, own) -> the scope a time event or unique call runs under
		coop_route.scope_now() -> the scope of the running code: the innermost wrapper's ("?" for an unknown one), nil outside
		coop_route.every(ms, fn) -> bool               fn() every ms (> 0) of time_global, routed as fn's own actor_on_update
		                                               handler; the same fn again changes its period
		coop_route.declare(name) -> bool               AddScriptCallback(name) unless it is declared already
		coop_route.handlers_from(callback, script) -> { fn, ... }   the handlers written in that script file, in priority order
		coop_route.domain_handlers(callback, domain) -> { fn, ... } what runs for a callback in that domain, as plain functions
		coop_route.print_status()                      hands over the counted runs; with the timing lines when any clock has calls
		coop_route.timing(on) -> bool                  opt-in timing of handlers and of the timing_sites of coop_mods_core, coop_mode;
		                                               on starts a new window
		coop_route.timing_reset()                      every clock back to zero, a new window
		coop_route.timing_top(n, by) -> { {group, key, calls, ms}, ... }   by "ms" (default) or "calls"
		coop_route.print_timing(n)                     the first n by time and by calls (docs\MEASURE.md)
--]]

local api = rawget(_G, "coop") -- engine namespace

local string_format = string.format
local tostring, tonumber, type, pairs, ipairs, pcall, select = tostring, tonumber, type, pairs, ipairs, pcall, select

local function log(fmt, ...)
	local ok, line = pcall(string_format, fmt, ...)
	printf("[coop-route] %s", ok and line or tostring(fmt))
end

local route -- coop.route once installed
local runs, note, note_batch -- coop.route.runs / note / note_batch
local scoped, scope -- coop.route.scoped / coop.route.scope; nil on an exe without them
local installed = false
local client = false -- this Lua state is a co-op client's (a client's state never becomes the host's)
local cb_table -- axr_main's local `intercepts`

-- Our wrapper objects -> true. Shared through _G by every load of this script: a second load in the same Lua state (a
-- script reload) wraps the first one's wrappers of callback_set and the globals, and sees the first one's handler wrappers
-- as done, so a handler is still guarded once.
local wrappers = rawget(_G, "_coop_route_wrappers")
if type(wrappers) ~= "table" then
	wrappers = setmetatable({}, { __mode = "k" })
	rawset(_G, "_coop_route_wrappers", wrappers)
end
local origin = setmetatable({}, { __mode = "k" }) -- wrapper -> { fn = handler, script, domain, callback }
local handler_dom = setmetatable({}, { __mode = "k" }) -- handler function (the method of an object) -> domain
local wrapped = {} -- callback -> { [handler] = wrapper }
local uc_wrap = setmetatable({}, { __mode = "kv" }) -- AddUniqueCall: function -> wrapper
local te_wrap = {} -- CreateTimeEvent: kept scope -> { function -> wrapper } (weak): one wrapper per function and scope
local te_route = setmetatable({}, { __mode = "k" }) -- CreateTimeEvent: function -> { domain, script } of its time_event route
local te_registrars = setmetatable({}, { __mode = "kv" }) -- registrar or its wrapper -> routed registrar
local uc_add_w, uc_rem_w -- our current replacements of the unique call globals

local counts = { host = 0, owner = 0, view = 0, none = 0, level = 0 } -- wrappers made, by domain
local stats = { rewrapped = 0, unrouted = 0, errors = 0 }
local err_seen = {} -- script -> Lua errors caught on this machine

-- The domains that run on every machine in every phase (routes::runs_here): their wrappers count their runs here, in
-- `ran`, and flush() hands the counts to the engine.
local EVERYWHERE = { owner = true, view = true }
local ran = { owner = 0, view = 0 }
local kRowMax = 1048576 -- the most calls one row of coop.route.note_batch counts (coop_router.cpp kNoteBatchMax)
local kFlushMs = 1000

local function flush()
	if not installed then return end
	local rows = {}
	for d, c in pairs(ran) do
		ran[d] = 0
		while c > 0 do
			local part = c < kRowMax and c or kRowMax
			rows[#rows + 1] = { d, "ran", part }
			c = c - part
		end
	end
	if rows[1] then note_batch(rows) end
end

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Timing (opt-in): calls and time of every routed handler and of the functions a watched module lists, for finding what
-- costs a frame.
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Off, nothing of it is on a call's path: a handler's wrapper tests one upvalue, every other function is the one the game
-- has. On, a wrapper calls its handler through a timed copy, and the functions the watched modules list in their
-- timing_sites (tbl -> { key -> label }) are swapped for timed copies until timing goes off. The clock is the engine's profile_timer (CPU ticks): a function's time includes what it
-- calls, a call inside a call of the same clock counts once in the time. A timed copy calls its function under pcall and
-- raises the same error again, so a failing function cannot leave its clock running.
local WATCHED = { { "coop_mods_core", "mods" }, { "coop_mode", "mode" } } -- module, clock group
local timing_on = false
local timing_since, timing_until -- time_global() of the window: switched on or reset, switched off
local clocks = {} -- group -> key -> { n = calls, t = profile_timer }
local swapped = {} -- { tbl, key, fn = the function it had, timed = its timed copy } while timing is on

local function clock(group, key)
	local g = clocks[group]
	if not g then
		g = {}
		clocks[group] = g
	end
	local c = g[key]
	if not c then
		c = { n = 0, t = profile_timer() }
		g[key] = c
	end
	return c
end

local function stopped(c, ok, ...)
	c.t:stop()
	if not ok then error((...), 0) end
	return ...
end

local function timed(c, f)
	return function(...)
		c.n = c.n + 1
		c.t:start()
		return stopped(c, pcall(f, ...))
	end
end

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Wrappers
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- A script that keeps failing is told to the player once, as news: a mod that fails every frame on a client leaves its
-- effect half done (an animation's item stays in the hands), which the player otherwise takes for a lost session.
local kTellAt = 30
local function tell(script, n)
	local tr = game and game.translate_string
	local fmt = tr and tr("st_coop_route_mod_failing")
	if not fmt or fmt == "st_coop_route_mod_failing" then
		fmt = "Co-op: the mod script %s keeps failing on this machine (%s errors) and does not work here. See the log."
	end
	local ok, text = pcall(string_format, fmt, tostring(script), tostring(n))
	local actor = db and db.actor
	if actor then pcall(actor.give_game_news, actor, "Anomaly Coop", ok and text or fmt, "ui_inGame2_Radiopomehi", 0, 15000) end
	log("! %s keeps failing on this client (%d errors), the player is told", tostring(script), n)
end

local function report(script, what, err)
	local n = (err_seen[script] or 0) + 1
	err_seen[script] = n
	stats.errors = stats.errors + 1
	if n <= 3 or n % 100 == 0 then
		log("! lua error in %s (%s), #%d, the session continues: %s", tostring(script), tostring(what), n, tostring(err))
	end
	if n == kTellAt then tell(script, n) end
end

local function finish(d, script, what, err_ret, ok, ...)
	if ok then return ... end
	note(d, "error", script)
	report(script, what, (...))
	return err_ret
end

-- A host script that makes things happen around "the player" (phantoms and psy hits around db.actor) runs its
-- actor_on_update as each player in turn - one call per tick, the identity rotating (coop_npc_players.with_identity), so
-- its own timers stay as they are and the clients get it too.
local ROTATE = { xcv_phantoms = true, xcv_phantoms_fix = true, more_dangerous_phantoms = true }
local turn = {}

local function as_player_in_turn(script, f, ...)
	if not api.is_active() then return f(...) end
	if not coop_mode.leads_here() then return end
	local P = rawget(_G, "coop_npc_players")
	if not (P and P.everyone and P.with_identity) then return end
	local list = P.everyone()
	local n = #list
	local i = turn[script] or 0
	for _ = 1, n do
		i = i % n + 1
		local who = list[i]
		if coop_mode.where(P.peer_of(who)) == "here" then
			turn[script] = i
			return P.with_identity(who, f, ...)
		end
	end
end

function _rotate_turn(script) return turn[script] end

-- A client's Lua state outlives its session. When the host is lost the engine has no session and says that every domain runs
-- (coop_router.cpp runs_now: single player). But the world of this state is still the host's MIRROR, with no simulation of its
-- own, and the host-side scripts fail on it (a squad's daily pass calls squad:update() on objects the mirror never set up).
-- What runs only on the host, or nowhere, in a session stays off in a client's state until that state is gone.
local ORPHAN_OFF = { host = true, level = true, none = true }
local function orphaned(d)
	if not (client and ORPHAN_OFF[d]) then return false end
	local ok, on = pcall(api.is_active)
	return not (ok and on == true)
end

-- The scope of the running code. The engine keeps every wrapper's domain (coop.route.scoped) and reads the scope from the
-- live Lua stack (coop.route.scope: the innermost registered wrapper), so a wrapper must stay on the stack while its handler runs.
local UNKNOWN_SCOPE = "?" -- a time event or unique call set by code of no known scope (coop_calls.h kUnknownScope)
local LEVELISH = { level = true, owner = true, view = true } -- domains that run on a map server too

local function mark(w, domain)
	if scoped then scoped(w, domain) end
end

-- the values of a call, returned by a frame that stays on the stack while it runs (not a tail call)
local function pass(...) return ... end

-- The scope a time event or a unique call runs under: the code that set it (`setter`, nil when it was no wrapper's), narrowed by
-- where its own function runs (`own`, its route): host where either is host (it runs only where the world is decided); the
-- setter's level, owner or view domain where the function also runs on a map server; else unknown.
function kept_scope(setter, own)
	if setter == "host" or own == "host" then return "host" end
	if LEVELISH[setter] and LEVELISH[own] then return setter end
	return UNKNOWN_SCOPE
end

-- The scope of the running code as the engine reads it: the innermost wrapper's domain, nil outside any (or without the engine API).
function scope_now()
	if not scope then return nil end
	local d = scope()
	return type(d) == "string" and d or nil
end

-- f runs where domain d runs. skip_ret: returned when it does not run here; err_ret: returned when its protected call
-- failed on a client.
local function guard(f, d, script, what, skip_ret, err_ret)
	local timed_f -- f through its clock, made when it first runs with timing on
	local everywhere = EVERYWHERE[d] == true
	local rotate = (d == "host" or d == "level") and what == "actor_on_update" and ROTATE[script]
	local w = function(...)
		if everywhere then
			ran[d] = ran[d] + 1
		elseif not runs(d) or orphaned(d) then
			note(d, "skipped", script, what) -- per (callback, script)
			return skip_ret
		else
			note(d, "ran")
		end
		local h = f
		if timing_on then
			timed_f = timed_f or timed(clock("route", what .. "@" .. script), f)
			h = timed_f
		end
		if client then
			if rotate then return finish(d, script, what, err_ret, pcall(as_player_in_turn, script, h, ...)) end
			return finish(d, script, what, err_ret, pcall(h, ...))
		end
		-- no tail call: this wrapper stays on the stack while the handler runs (its scope); no pcall: the stock behaviour
		if rotate then return pass(as_player_in_turn(script, h, ...)) end
		return pass(h(...))
	end
	wrappers[w] = true
	mark(w, d)
	return w
end

local source = setmetatable({}, { __mode = "k" })

function inherit_source(wrapper, original)
	source[wrapper] = source[original] or original
end

-- domain, script of f for a callback; nil when the engine does not answer (the handler then stays as it is)
local function route_of(callback, f)
	local ok, d, script = pcall(route.of, callback, source[f] or f)
	if ok and type(d) == "string" and counts[d] then return d, script or "?" end
	stats.unrouted = stats.unrouted + 1
	return nil
end

local function remember(w, fu, d, script, callback)
	origin[w] = { fn = fu, script = script, domain = d, callback = callback }
	counts[d] = counts[d] + 1
end

-- The object to register instead of the handler fu of callback `name` (fu itself if it cannot be routed).
local function wrap_entry(name, fu)
	if wrappers[fu] then return fu end
	local m = wrapped[name]
	local w = m and m[fu]
	if w then return w end
	local tf = type(fu)
	local f = fu
	if tf == "table" or tf == "userdata" then
		-- axr_main convention: an object with a method named like the callback, called as obj[name](obj, ...)
		local ok, method = pcall(function() return fu[name] end)
		if not (ok and type(method) == "function") then return fu end
		f = method
	elseif tf ~= "function" then
		return fu
	end
	local d, script = route_of(name, f)
	if not d then return fu end
	handler_dom[f] = d
	if tf == "function" then
		w = guard(fu, d, script, name, nil, nil)
	else
		local g = guard(function(...)
			local mm = fu[name]
			if mm then return mm(fu, ...) end
		end, d, script, name, nil, nil)
		w = {}
		w[name] = function(_, ...) return g(...) end
		wrappers[w] = true
	end
	remember(w, fu, d, script, name)
	if not m then
		m = {}
		wrapped[name] = m
	end
	m[fu] = w
	return w
end

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- axr_main
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
local function find_upvalue(fn, wanted)
	if type(fn) ~= "function" then return nil end
	for i = 1, 255 do
		local n, v = debug.getupvalue(fn, i)
		if n == nil then return nil end
		if n == wanted then return v end
	end
	return nil
end

-- axr_main's local callback table: an upvalue of callback_set (before it is wrapped) or of make_callback.
function intercepts()
	if cb_table then return cb_table end
	local am = rawget(_G, "axr_main") or axr_main
	if type(am) ~= "table" then return nil end
	local t = find_upvalue(am.callback_set, "intercepts") or find_upvalue(am.make_callback, "intercepts")
	if type(t) == "table" then cb_table = t end
	return cb_table
end

-- Handlers registered before install: each is replaced in place by its wrapper, with the same priority value.
local function rewrap_registered(t)
	for name, list in pairs(t) do
		if type(list) == "table" then
			local found = {}
			for fu, v in pairs(list) do
				if not wrappers[fu] then
					found[#found + 1] = fu
					found[#found + 1] = v
				end
			end
			for i = 1, #found, 2 do
				local fu, v = found[i], found[i + 1]
				local w = wrap_entry(name, fu)
				if w ~= fu then
					list[fu] = nil
					list[w] = v
					stats.rewrapped = stats.rewrapped + 1
				end
			end
		end
	end
end

local function wrap_axr()
	local am = rawget(_G, "axr_main") or axr_main
	if not (type(am) == "table" and type(am.callback_set) == "function" and type(am.callback_unset) == "function") then
		log("! axr_main.callback_set not found: script callbacks are not routed on this machine")
		return
	end
	local t = intercepts() -- before callback_set is replaced (its upvalues lead to the table)
	if t then
		rewrap_registered(t)
	else
		log("! axr_main's callback table not reachable: handlers registered before the shim are not routed")
	end
	local orig_set, orig_unset = am.callback_set, am.callback_unset
	am.callback_set = function(name, fu)
		if fu == nil or (t and t[name] == nil) then return orig_set(name, fu) end -- axr_main reports those
		return orig_set(name, wrap_entry(name, fu))
	end
	am.callback_unset = function(name, fu)
		local m = wrapped[name]
		if fu ~= nil and m then
			local w = m[fu]
			if w then
				m[fu] = nil
				return orig_unset(name, w)
			end
			local o = origin[fu] -- the wrapper itself (an addon that read the table)
			if o and o.callback == name then m[o.fn] = nil end
		end
		return orig_unset(name, fu)
	end
end

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- AddUniqueCall / RemoveUniqueCall / CreateTimeEvent
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
local function wrap_once(cache, f, what, skip_ret)
	local w = cache[f]
	if w then return w end
	local d, script = route_of(what, f)
	if not d then return nil end
	handler_dom[f] = d
	w = guard(f, d, script, what, skip_ret, true) -- a broken one returns true: not called again
	cache[f] = w
	remember(w, f, d, script, what)
	return w
end

-- AddUniqueCall: one wrapper per function (the call is unique by its function), its scope the one kept from the code that added
-- it last.
local function wrap_unique(f)
	local w = wrap_once(uc_wrap, f, "unique_call", false)
	if w then mark(w, kept_scope(scope_now(), origin[w].domain)) end
	return w
end

-- CreateTimeEvent: one wrapper per function and kept scope (time events are keyed by their ids, not by the function), routed by the
-- function's own script; a broken one returns true: it is removed.
local function wrap_time_event(f)
	local r = te_route[f]
	if not r then
		local d, script = route_of("time_event", f)
		if not d then return nil end
		r = { d, script }
		te_route[f] = r
	end
	local d, script = r[1], r[2]
	local kept = kept_scope(scope_now(), d)
	local cache = te_wrap[kept]
	if not cache then
		cache = setmetatable({}, { __mode = "kv" })
		te_wrap[kept] = cache
	end
	local w = cache[f]
	if w then return w end
	handler_dom[f] = d
	w = guard(f, d, script, "time_event", true, true)
	mark(w, kept)
	cache[f] = w
	remember(w, f, d, script, "time_event")
	return w
end

local function wrap_time_event_registrar(create)
	local w = te_registrars[create]
	if not w then
		w = function(ev_id, act_id, timer, f, ...)
			if type(f) == "function" and not wrappers[f] then
				f = wrap_time_event(f) or f
			end
			return create(ev_id, act_id, timer, f, ...)
		end
		te_registrars[create], te_registrars[w] = w, w
	end
	return w
end

-- Keep Crafting Window Opened copies the module registrar into its script environments.
local function wrap_demonized_time_events()
	local events = rawget(_G, "demonized_time_events")
	if events == nil and type(getFS) == "function" and getFS():exist("$game_scripts$", "demonized_time_events.script") then
		events = _G.demonized_time_events
	end
	local create = type(events) == "table" and rawget(events, "CreateTimeEvent")
	if type(create) ~= "function" then return end
	local routed = wrap_time_event_registrar(create)
	events.CreateTimeEvent = routed
	for _, name in ipairs({
		"zz_item_cooking_keep_crafting_window_open",
		"zz_item_repair_keep_crafting_window_open",
		"zz_ui_workshop_keep_crafting_window_open",
	}) do
		local patch = rawget(_G, name)
		if type(patch) == "table" and te_registrars[rawget(patch, "CreateTimeEvent")] == routed then
			patch.CreateTimeEvent = routed
		end
	end
end

-- Later scripts can replace the globals. Wrap the current versions with the same routing rules.
local function wrap_globals()
	local add = rawget(_G, "AddUniqueCall")
	if type(add) == "function" and add ~= uc_add_w then
		uc_add_w = function(f)
			if type(f) ~= "function" or wrappers[f] then return add(f) end
			return add(wrap_unique(f) or f)
		end
		_G.AddUniqueCall = uc_add_w
	end
	local rem = rawget(_G, "RemoveUniqueCall")
	if type(rem) == "function" and rem ~= uc_rem_w then
		uc_rem_w = function(f)
			return rem((f ~= nil and uc_wrap[f]) or f)
		end
		_G.RemoveUniqueCall = uc_rem_w
	end
	local cte = rawget(_G, "CreateTimeEvent")
	if type(cte) == "function" then _G.CreateTimeEvent = wrap_time_event_registrar(cte) end
	wrap_demonized_time_events()
end

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Tick service
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
local ticks = {} -- { fn, ms, due, w = fn's wrapper (fn itself without the router) }, in the order every() first saw them
local tick_of = {} -- fn -> its entry
local tick_runner_on = false

-- one clock read per frame; an entry's wrapper is called only when it is due
local function run_ticks()
	local now = time_global()
	for i = 1, #ticks do
		local e = ticks[i]
		if now >= e.due then
			e.due = now + e.ms
			e.w()
		end
	end
end
wrappers[run_ticks] = true -- registered as it is: each tick runs through its own wrapper

local function tick_wrapper(fn)
	if not installed then return fn end
	local d, script = route_of("actor_on_update", fn)
	if not d then return fn end
	handler_dom[fn] = d
	local w = guard(fn, d, script, "actor_on_update", nil, nil)
	remember(w, fn, d, script, "actor_on_update")
	return w
end

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- API
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
function install()
	if installed then return true end
	local r = api and api.route
	if not (type(r) == "table" and type(r.of) == "function" and type(r.runs) == "function"
		and type(r.note) == "function" and type(r.note_batch) == "function") then
		log("! coop.route missing (an exe without the callback router): callbacks are not routed")
		return false
	end
	route, runs, note, note_batch = r, r.runs, r.note, r.note_batch
	if type(r.scoped) == "function" and type(r.scope) == "function" then scoped, scope = r.scoped, r.scope end
	local ok, is_client = pcall(api.is_client)
	client = ok and is_client == true
	installed = true
	wrap_axr()
	wrap_globals()
	for i = 1, #ticks do -- a tick taken before the router was there gets the wrapper its registration would have
		ticks[i].w = tick_wrapper(ticks[i].fn)
	end
	log("installed on a %s: %d handler(s) registered earlier routed in place", client and "client" or "host or single player",
		stats.rewrapped)
	return true
end

-- Runs fn every ms milliseconds through the wrapper its own actor_on_update registration would get. A script may ask
-- while it is being loaded: install() routes the ticks taken before it. A tick is not a handler axr_main lists:
-- handlers_of, handlers_from and domain_handlers do not return it.
function every(ms, fn)
	if type(ms) ~= "number" or not (ms > 0) or type(fn) ~= "function" then return false end
	local e = tick_of[fn]
	if e then
		e.ms = ms
		return true
	end
	e = { fn = fn, ms = ms, due = 0, w = tick_wrapper(fn) }
	tick_of[fn] = e
	ticks[#ticks + 1] = e
	return true
end

-- axr_main calls every script's on_game_start after all scripts are loaded: a CreateTimeEvent replaced since install
-- (_g_patches.script) is wrapped again.
function on_game_start()
	if installed then
		wrap_globals()
		every(kFlushMs, flush)
	end
	if not tick_runner_on then
		tick_runner_on = true
		RegisterScriptCallback("actor_on_update", run_ticks)
	end
end

local function by_priority(a, b) return a.v < b.v end

-- What pick(key) gives for each handler of a callback that it keeps (key: what axr_main's table holds, a wrapper or a
-- handler; nil drops it), in the order axr_main calls them.
local function sorted_handlers(callback, pick)
	local out = {}
	local t = intercepts()
	local list = t and t[callback]
	if type(list) ~= "table" then return out end
	local found = {}
	for k, v in pairs(list) do
		local fn = pick(k)
		if fn ~= nil then found[#found + 1] = { fn = fn, v = tonumber(v) or 0 } end
	end
	table.sort(found, by_priority)
	for i, e in ipairs(found) do out[i] = e.fn end
	return out
end

function handlers_of(callback, script)
	if type(script) ~= "string" then return {} end
	script = script:lower()
	return sorted_handlers(callback, function(k)
		local o = origin[k]
		local fn, sc = k, nil
		if o then
			fn, sc = o.fn, o.script
		elseif route then -- registered while it could not be routed: ask now
			local f = k
			if type(k) ~= "function" then
				local okm, mm = pcall(function() return k[callback] end)
				f = okm and mm or nil
			end
			if type(f) == "function" then sc = select(2, route_of(callback, f)) end
		end
		if sc == script then return fn end
		return nil
	end)
end

-- Declares a script callback unless somebody has declared it already. axr_main's callback_add prints a warning with a stack
-- traceback for a second declaration, and which script comes first is not ours to say: game_fast_travel declares
-- on_before_fast_travel in its file body, two of our scripts declare coop_faction_changed. true when it was declared here.
function declare(name)
	if type(name) ~= "string" or name == "" then return false end
	local t = intercepts()
	if type(t) == "table" and t[name] ~= nil then return false end
	local add = rawget(_G, "AddScriptCallback")
	if type(add) ~= "function" then return false end
	add(name)
	return true
end

-- The handlers of a callback that were written in the script `script`, told by the function's own source: a handler no rule
-- routes is found too. In the order axr_main calls them, and as their script registered them (never the wrapper).
-- coop_game_settings runs game_difficulties' on_game_load once more on a new world with it.
function handlers_from(callback, script)
	if type(script) ~= "string" or script == "" then return {} end
	local tail = script:lower() .. ".script"
	return sorted_handlers(callback, function(k)
		local o = origin[k]
		local fn = o and o.fn or k
		if type(fn) ~= "function" then return nil end
		local ok, info = pcall(debug.getinfo, fn, "S")
		local src = ok and type(info) == "table" and type(info.source) == "string" and info.source:lower() or ""
		local before = #src - #tail
		if before >= 0 and src:sub(before + 1) == tail and (before == 0 or src:sub(before, before):find("[\\/@]") ~= nil) then
			return fn
		end
		return nil
	end)
end

-- What runs for a callback in that domain, as plain functions: a handler object is called as axr_main calls it. The death relay
-- (coop_srv_quests_far) tells these by hand: on the world server they did not hear the death of a map server's level.
function domain_handlers(callback, domain)
	return sorted_handlers(callback, function(k)
		local o = origin[k]
		if not (o and o.domain == domain) then return nil end
		local fu = o.fn
		if type(fu) == "function" then return fu end
		return function(...)
			local m = fu[callback]
			if m then return m(fu, ...) end
		end
	end)
end

function wrapper_of(callback, fn)
	local m = wrapped[callback]
	return m and fn ~= nil and m[fn] or nil
end

function script_of(fn)
	if not route then return "?" end
	local ok, _, script = pcall(route.of, "", source[fn] or fn)
	return ok and script or "?"
end

-- The domain of a function registered as a handler (an axr_main callback, a unique call, a time event, a tick of every),
-- nil for any other function: coop_core_world's news reads it for the handler it finds on the stack.
function handler_domain(fn)
	return fn ~= nil and handler_dom[fn] or nil
end

function print_status()
	flush()
	local caught = 0
	for _, n in pairs(err_seen) do caught = caught + n end
	log("shim %s on this %s | wrappers host %d, owner %d, view %d, none %d, level %d | routed in place %d, not routed %d, "
		.. "lua errors caught %d", installed and "on" or "off", client and "client" or "host or single player",
		counts.host, counts.owner, counts.view, counts.none, counts.level, stats.rewrapped, stats.unrouted, caught)
	if next(clocks) then print_timing(10) end
end

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Timing: switch, report
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
local function get(tbl, key) return tbl[key] end
local function set(tbl, key, v) tbl[key] = v end

local function swap_in(tbl, key, group, label)
	local ok, fn = pcall(get, tbl, key)
	if not (ok and type(fn) == "function") then return end
	local copy = timed(clock(group, label), fn)
	if pcall(set, tbl, key, copy) then swapped[#swapped + 1] = { tbl = tbl, key = key, fn = fn, timed = copy } end
end

local function swap_all()
	for _, w in ipairs(WATCHED) do
		local m = rawget(_G, w[1])
		local sites = type(m) == "table" and rawget(m, "timing_sites")
		if type(sites) == "table" then
			for tbl, keys in pairs(sites) do
				for key, label in pairs(keys) do swap_in(tbl, key, w[2], label) end
			end
		end
	end
end

-- A function somebody replaced again while timing was on keeps the replacement (its timed copy then stays inside it).
local function swap_out()
	for i = #swapped, 1, -1 do
		local s = swapped[i]
		local ok, now = pcall(get, s.tbl, s.key)
		if ok and now == s.timed then pcall(set, s.tbl, s.key, s.fn) end
		swapped[i] = nil
	end
end

local function now_ms()
	local tg = rawget(_G, "time_global")
	return tg and tg() or nil
end

-- timing(true | false) -> whether timing is on now; timing() only asks. On starts a new window (timing_reset). Off without the
-- engine's profile_timer.
function timing(on)
	if on == nil then return timing_on end
	on = on == true
	if on == timing_on then return timing_on end
	if on then
		if type(rawget(_G, "profile_timer")) ~= "function" then
			log("! timing: no profile_timer in this exe")
			return false
		end
		timing_on = true
		timing_reset()
		swap_all()
	else
		timing_on = false
		timing_until = now_ms()
		swap_out()
	end
	log("timing %s", timing_on and "on" or "off")
	return timing_on
end

-- Every clock back to zero, kept where a timed copy holds it.
function timing_reset()
	for _, g in pairs(clocks) do
		for _, c in pairs(g) do
			c.n = 0
			c.t = profile_timer()
		end
	end
	timing_since = now_ms()
	timing_until = nil
end

-- The clocks with calls, the first n by "ms" (total time, the default) or by "calls": { {group, key, calls, ms}, ... }
function timing_top(n, by)
	local list = {}
	for group, g in pairs(clocks) do
		for key, c in pairs(g) do
			if c.n > 0 then list[#list + 1] = { group = group, key = key, calls = c.n, ms = c.t:time() / 1000 } end
		end
	end
	local field = by == "calls" and "calls" or "ms"
	table.sort(list, function(a, b)
		if a[field] ~= b[field] then return a[field] > b[field] end
		if a.group ~= b.group then return a.group < b.group end
		return a.key < b.key
	end)
	for i = #list, (tonumber(n) or #list) + 1, -1 do list[i] = nil end
	return list
end

-- The first n by time and the first n by calls, one line each (a log line stays short: the engine's log ends the process on
-- a line over 4 KB).
function print_timing(n)
	n = tonumber(n) or 10
	local till = timing_until or now_ms()
	local secs = till and timing_since and (till - timing_since) / 1000 or 0
	log("timing %s, window %.1f s", timing_on and "on" or "off", secs)
	for _, by in ipairs({ "ms", "calls" }) do
		for i, e in ipairs(timing_top(n, by)) do
			local per_s = secs > 0 and string_format(", %.3f ms/s, %.1f calls/s", e.ms / secs, e.calls / secs) or ""
			log("timing by %s #%d %s %s: %.3f ms, %d calls, %.2f us/call%s", by, i, e.group, e.key, e.ms, e.calls,
				e.ms * 1000 / e.calls, per_s)
		end
	end
end
